    "text": "A refrigerator keeps food cold by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. Inside the fridge, it absorbs heat and turns into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. Next, the hot gas flows through coils behind or under the fridge, where it releases heat into your kitchen and turns back into a liquid. Finally, it cycles back inside to soak up more heat. Because heat is constantly removed, the inside stays cold, slowing down bacteria so your food lasts longer."
